BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:28b22be9e0df3b2a57e60c2a@visitsouthbend.com
DTSTAMP:20260825T080852Z
SUMMARY:LECTURE—"AMERICAN ODYSSEY: WHAT AN ANCIENT STORY REVEALS ABOUT OU
 R DIVIDED SOULS"
LOCATION:University of Notre Dame
DESCRIPTION:The ancient story of The Odyssey addresses fundamentally human 
 questions of home\, ambition\, vengeance\, and mercy. Yet as political ph
 ilosopher Patrick Deneen reveals\, it is also the most American of classi
 cal texts. Like Odysseus\, the American identity is defined by a core ten
 sion: a simultaneous commitment to the comfort of home and the drive to l
 eave it behind. In this compelling presentation\, Deneen uses the lens of
  Western inheritance to unpack the divided soul of the American character
 . Arriving alongside the U. S. semi-quincentennial\, his book offers a ti
 mely look at how an ancient Greek epic continues to shape our greatest st
 ories\, our cultural politics\, and our national identity today. We will 
 begin our evening with Prof. Deneen with book sales and signing at 6:15 p
 .m. ET in the atrium of the Eck Visitors Center. Following the conclusion
  of Prof. Deneen's remarks at 8:15 p.m.\, all event attendees are welcome
  to join us for a light reception. Patrick J. Deneen holds a B. A. in Eng
 lish literature and a Ph. D. in political science from Rutgers University
 . From 1995–97 he was speechwriter and special advisor to the director 
 of the United States Information Agency. From 1997–2005 he was assistan
 t professor of politics at Princeton University. From 2005–2012 he was 
 the Tsakopoulos-Kounalakis Associate Professor of Government at Georgetow
 n University\, before joining the faculty of Notre Dame in fall 2012. He 
 is the author and editor of several books and numerous articles and revie
 ws and has delivered invited lectures around the world. Deneen was awarde
 d the APSA's Leo Strauss Award for Best Dissertation in Political Theory 
 in 1995\, and an honorable mention for the APSA's Best First Book Award i
 n 2000. He has been awarded research fellowships from Princeton Universit
 y\, Earhart Foundation\, and the Institute for Advanced Study at the Univ
 ersity of Virginia. His teaching and writing interests focus on the histo
 ry of political thought\, American political thought\, liberalism\, conse
 rvatism\, and constitutionalism. His widely-discussed 2018 book\, Why Lib
 eralism Failed\, has been translated into over twenty languages and was p
 raised by former President Barack Obama. Originally published at constudi
 es.nd.edu.\n\nMore Info: https://www.visitsouthbend.com/events/lecture-am
 erican-odyssey-what-an-ancient-story-reveals-about-our-divided-souls/
URL:https://www.visitsouthbend.com/events/lecture-american-odyssey-what-an-
 ancient-story-reveals-about-our-divided-souls/
DTSTART;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20260827T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20260827T201500
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
